import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const photoCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '4.136e-15*x - 2.28', label: 'max electron energy (sodium)', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '0', label: 'zero-energy line', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.4)', width: 1.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 1.5e15, label: 'light frequency f (Hz)' },
  y: { min: -3, max: 4, label: 'electron energy (eV)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/quantum-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum mechanics begins with an insult to common sense: <strong>light — and everything else
    — comes in discrete packets</strong>. The photoelectric effect proved it in 1905: light below
    a threshold frequency ejects <em>no</em> electrons from a metal, no matter how bright.
    Brightness (many dim packets) can't beat frequency (each packet's energy E = hf). Einstein's
    explanation won the Nobel — and the packet idea became the quantum.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the photoelectric plot — the crossing marks the threshold frequency; below it, nothing happens, ever" widget="plotter" config={photoCfg} />

  <p>
    The line's slope is Planck's constant (h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s = 4.136 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s) — the
    universal exchange rate between energy and frequency. The crossing with zero is the
    work function: 2.28 eV for sodium, corresponding to blue-green light. Red light, however
    intense, never frees an electron. That's the graph that started the quantum revolution.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules of the small world</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wave–particle duality:</strong> electrons fired one at a time through a double slit build an interference pattern — each electron explores both paths. Matter and light both behave as waves that arrive as packets.</li>
    <li><strong>Heisenberg's uncertainty (1927):</strong> position × momentum ≥ ħ/2 — not a measurement limitation but a fact of nature. Sharply localised waves must be built from many momenta.</li>
    <li><strong>The wavefunction:</strong> a state's wave encodes probability amplitudes; |ψ|² gives the odds of finding the particle here. Quantum theory predicts probabilities perfectly — and insists that's all it predicts.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantised atoms:</strong> electrons occupy standing-wave orbitals; photons are emitted at exact energy differences. One idea, and the entire periodic table's spectral fingerprint bookkeeping makes sense.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Planck (1900); Einstein (1905); Heisenberg (1927); Feynman Lectures Vol. III.</p>
</TopicPage>
